Wildcards — question mark extends T.

Here's the fact that surprises everyone: a list of integer is not a list of number — even though an integer is a number. Generics are invariant. A parameterised type is not a subtype of the same type with a supertype argument. So a method that takes a list of number will reject a list of integer, which makes it uselessly rigid. Wildcards fix this, and question-mark-extends is the first half.

First, why does invariance even exist? Because it's protecting you. Suppose the assignment were allowed. You have a list of integer. You assign it to a list of number — imagine that compiled. Now you add three point one four, a double, into what is really a list of integer. Then you read index zero back as an integer — and boom, it isn't one. Invariance forbids that assignment precisely to stop the disaster that follows. So the strictness is a feature. But it does mean a plain list-of-number parameter can't accept the subtype lists you'd reasonably want to pass.

The fix is an upper-bounded wildcard. You write the parameter as list of question-mark-extends-number — meaning "a list of some unknown type that is a number, or a subtype of number." Now every list of integer, list of double, and list of number is accepted. So a sum method taking a list of question-mark-extends-number can iterate it, reading each element as a number and calling double-value, and total them up. You can pass any list whose element type is a number. And reading works, because whatever the real element type is, it is a number.

But there's a catch: you can read, but you can't write. With question-mark-extends, the compiler knows only that the elements are some subtype of number — but not which one — so it forbids adding. You can get index zero as a number, fine. But calling add of one is a compile error, because the compiler can't be sure whether the list is really integers, or doubles, or something else. The one exception is null, which is a member of every type. If the list were really a list of integer, adding a double would corrupt it; and since the compiler can't tell, it bans all additions. That's the defining trade-off: question-mark-extends makes a source you can read from, not a sink you can write to.

So here's the mental model: think of question-mark-extends-T as declaring "this parameter is a producer of T's." Data flows out of it, into your method. You iterate it, sum it, copy from it. But you cannot put anything in, except null, because the exact type is unknown. And this is exactly why collections dot max, and stream dot map, are written with question-mark-extends on their input collections — they only read the elements. So use question-mark-extends whenever your method consumes a collection's contents without adding to it. The mirror image — a parameter you write into — needs question-mark-super. And together, they form the rule every Java developer memorises: P-E-C-S, which is next.
